A var an imported package declares is declared by the program that reads it.

passLibFuncs promotes the funcs another package declares when a body this
build emits calls them, because every backend emits from the program's own
lists. A promoted func reading its own package's state took nothing with it:
`calls += 1` in `bump` named a var no target declared -- undefined on every Go
target, a ReferenceError on html, and the text reading it drawn empty there.
The var is promoted beside the funcs now, before reactivity, so it is state
like the program's own and the text follows it.

Emitting `bump` also found a second defect, on fyne alone: a zero-parameter
func returning a value was taken for a computed, and a computed's body is
built by the getter emitter, which does not translate the updater a write
needs -- `__n0.Text = …`, a field no Go type has. A func that writes state is
an action and is no longer one.

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import doc "./doc"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="t") {
    doc.article
}
-- doc/article.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

var calls = 0

func bump() int {
    calls += 1
    return calls
}

component article ui.node {
    ui.vbox {
        ui.button(text="b", @click { bump() })
        ui.text(value="calls {calls}")
    }
}
